<title>FRANCE: Volkswagen sees Seat breakeven by 1997.</title>
<headline>Volkswagen sees Seat breakeven by 1997.</headline>
<dateline>PARIS 1996-10-02</dateline>
<text>
<p>Volkswagen AG management board chairman Ferdinand Piech said in an interview on Wednesday that he expected the Skoda unit to make money this year and Seat to break even in 1997.</p>
<p>Piech said the Volkswagen group expected to sell four million cars worldwide in 1996, compared with 3.57 million in 1995.</p>
<p>&quot;Our market share in Europe -- 17.2 percent in the first half of 1996 -- should increase by one point,&quot; he told the French newspaper La Tribune.</p>
<p>&quot;Seat will break even at the latest next year. And Skoda will make money in 1996,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>Piech also said the group aimed to reduce the assembly time on small cars by around half to seven hours to be fully competitive against Asian carmakers.</p>
<p>-- Paris editorial, + 33 1 4221 5452</p>
</text>
